Chapter: ListViewBounds
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A tail view is bounded by itself, not by the block behind it. The view
 carries its OWN length at offset 0 and a phantom base at [p+8], so a
 guard that read the length off the effective base would bound a view by
 the backing block -- which is longer, and which is where the elements
 before the view live. The `view:` walk and `view-len` together say the
 view is bounded by itself.

 These two rows lived in `list-index-bounds` until 2026-07-28. They are
 split out because `tail-of` matches Cons on a builtin List, and that
 desugar targets the `__list-len`/`__list-head`/`__list-tail` intrinsics
 the cross plugs refuse by design -- so the two rows carried the file's
 six legal-index readings off both cross lanes with them. Here they
 carry only themselves; the `.cross-refusal` sidecar pins the refusal
 as the tested behavior.

Section: Report

  walk-at : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  walk-at (xs) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else walk-at xs (i + 1) n (acc & " " & show (list-at xs i))

  read-all : List Integer -> Text
  read-all (xs) = walk-at xs 0 (list-length xs) ""

  tail-of : List Integer -> List Integer
  tail-of (xs) = when xs
    is Cons (h) (t) -> t
    is otherwise -> xs

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("view:" & read-all (tail-of [1, 2, 3, 4]))
    print-line-uni ("view-len=" & show (list-length (tail-of [1, 2, 3, 4])))
  end

